==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|aspersiō||sprinkling}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An attack on somebody's [[reputation]] or good name, often in the phrase ''to cast aspersions upon&hellip;''.
#* {{quote-book|year=1799|year_published=1906|author=[[w:Pennsylvania House of Representatives|Pennsylvania House of Representatives]]|title=[[s:State Documents on Federal Relations|State Documents on Federal Relations]]|chapter=[[s:State Documents on Federal Relations/10|Resolutions of the House of Representatives of Pennsylvania to Kentucky]]|publisher=University of Pennsylvania|page=[[s:Page:State Documents on Federal Relations.djvu/35|21]]|passage=No criminality can be infered or punishment inflicted, but for writing, printing, uttering, or publishing false, scandalous and malicious '''aspersions''' against the government.}}
# {{label|en|obsolete}} A [[sprinkling]] of [[Wikipedia:holy water|holy water]].
#* '''1610''', ''[[s:The Tempest|The Tempest]]'', by [[w:William Shakespeare|Shakespeare]], act 4 scene 1
#*: If thou dost break her virgin knot before
#*: All sanctimonious ceremonies may
#*: With full and holy rite be minister'd,
#*: No sweet '''aspersion''' shall the heavens let fall
#*: To make this contract grow; but barren hate [...]

